{
  "image_name": "0d389c77cf496b9359037900e6954507.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how early tumors are either controlled by immunity or progress by escaping it, and how this balance might be shifted back toward regression. In the top schematic, a premalignant lesion contains a few abnormal tumor cells among normal cells. In many people, immune surveillance removes these cells, often after dying tumor cells release inflammatory material such as DNA. In a smaller subset, however, the lesion appears to evade immunity and expand toward clinical disease. The visible factors linked to escape include tumor-associated inflammation, metabolic changes, increased amino acid breakdown, and immune checkpoint pathways.\n\nThe lower schematic connects this process to the draining lymph node. Tumor-derived inflammatory signals seem to reach antigen-presenting cells (APCs), which can either support productive T-cell activation or become regulatory/suppressive. In the suppressive state, regulatory APCs, Treg cells, and checkpoints such as PD1 or CTLA4 inhibit helper/effector T cells. This immunosuppressive node environment favors tumor persistence. The right side shows the therapeutic idea: block Treg cells and checkpoints while boosting stimulatory APCs and effector T cells. That enhanced antitumor immunity is linked to the top outcome of tumor regression rather than progression.",
